---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHeader from '../../components/PageHeader.astro';
import Tldr from '../../components/Tldr.astro';
import TryItNow from '../../components/TryItNow.astro';
import CopyPrompt from '../../components/CopyPrompt.astro';
import KeyTakeaways from '../../components/KeyTakeaways.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';
import Quote from '../../components/Quote.astro';
import { href } from '../../lib/site';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Claude in Chrome"
  description="Claude works directly in your browser — navigating pages, filling forms, and pulling data from sites that don't have a connector."
  pageId="chrome"
  readMinutes={7}
>
  <PageHeader
    icon="🌐"
    title="Claude in Chrome"
    subtitle="For most of this training, using Claude has meant opening a conversation. This is Claude leaving the chat window — and showing up inside the browser where the work already happens."
    readMinutes={7}
  />

  <Tldr>
    <p>
      Claude can now work <strong>directly in your Chrome browser</strong> — reading pages,
      clicking, filling forms, and stepping through multi-page workflows on your behalf. It's built
      for the tools that <em>don't</em> have a
      <a class="text-gold underline underline-offset-2" href={href('/connectors/')}>connector</a> — the vendor
      portal, the SaaS dashboard, the supplier site. Same golden rules apply: Claude acts as you, it
      confirms before anything consequential, and the
      <a class="text-gold underline underline-offset-2" href={href('/safety/')}>Safety &amp; Data</a> rules
      still hold.
    </p>
  </Tldr>

  <div class="prose-content">
    <p>
      Claude in Chrome is a browser extension that sits in a side panel while you browse. It sees the
      page you're on, and — when you ask — it can click buttons, fill out forms, move between tabs, and
      work through multi-step tasks for you. Anthropic sums it up plainly: Claude can read, click, and
      navigate websites right in your browser.
    </p>
    <p>
      Here's the mental model that matters: <strong>this is different from pasting a URL into a
      chat.</strong> When you drop a link into Chat, Claude reads a snapshot of that one page. In
      Chrome, Claude is <em>operating the browser</em> — it can work inside a dashboard you're already
      signed into, page through results, compare several open tabs, and take action on live pages. It's
      the difference between describing a website to a colleague and handing them your keyboard.
    </p>
    <p>
      It's also distinct from the
      <a class="text-gold underline underline-offset-2" href={href('/connectors/')}>connectors</a> you learned
      about earlier. A connector talks to a tool through its API — that's how Claude reads Jira or Ramp.
      Chrome is for everything <em>without</em> a clean API: the dashboard nobody's wired up, the vendor
      portal, the government form, the supplier site. If you can do it in a browser tab, Claude can help
      you do it there.
    </p>
    <p>
      You can also choose which model handles a Chrome task — the same
      <a class="text-gold underline underline-offset-2" href={href('/models/')}>model-choice</a> instinct you
      use everywhere else. Reach for a heavier model when a workflow is complex or high-stakes, and a
      faster one for quick, routine steps. Model choice is your first dial here too.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 class="mb-3 mt-10 text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">What you'd actually use it for</h2>

  <div class="table-shell reveal my-4">
    <table class="data-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Team</th>
          <th>Try asking Claude in Chrome</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td class="font-semibold">🧾 Finance</td>
          <td class="text-muted">"Log into this vendor portal, pull our last six invoices into a table, and flag any that don't match our PO numbers."</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="font-semibold">🔧 Engineering</td>
          <td class="text-muted">"Compare the spec sheets open in these three supplier tabs and give me a side-by-side of lead time, min order qty, and price."</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="font-semibold">🏭 Manufacturing / Quality</td>
          <td class="text-muted">"Fill out this supplier corrective-action web form using the details I'll paste, then stop before submitting so I can review."</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="font-semibold">📣 Marketing</td>
          <td class="text-muted">"Go through these five competitor pages and summarize how each one is positioning range and price."</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="font-semibold">💻 Ops / IT</td>
          <td class="text-muted">"Pull the open ticket counts from this dashboard across all four tabs and total them by category."</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="prose-content">
    A few of the higher-leverage moves worth knowing:
  </p>

  <div class="grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-3 my-5">
    <div class="hover-lift rounded-[12px] border border-border bg-card p-4">
      <p class="mb-1 font-semibold text-text">Multi-tab workflows</p>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed">Drag a set of tabs into Claude's tab group and it can read and work across all of them at once — no manual switching to compile information from six places.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="hover-lift rounded-[12px] border border-border bg-card p-4">
      <p class="mb-1 font-semibold text-text">Record a workflow</p>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed">For something you do the same way every week, hit record, walk through the steps once, and save it as a shortcut. Claude learns to repeat it — teaching by showing instead of describing.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="hover-lift rounded-[12px] border border-border bg-card p-4">
      <p class="mb-1 font-semibold text-text">Scheduled tasks</p>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed">Set a browser task to run on its own — daily, weekly, monthly. Great for a Monday-morning dashboard pull that lands before you're even at your desk.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <TryItNow title="Try it — low stakes to start">
    <p>
      Open a few articles or product pages in tabs, drag them into a group, and ask Claude to pull the
      key facts into one comparison table. It's a safe, read-only way to feel out how the side panel
      works before you point it at anything that <em>writes</em> data.
    </p>
    <CopyPrompt text="I've grouped a few tabs. Read all of them and build a single comparison table of the key facts — one row per page, columns for the details that actually differ. Don't click or submit anything." />
  </TryItNow>

  <h2 class="mb-3 mt-10 text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">⚠️ The guardrails (read this part)</h2>

  <div class="prose-content">
    <p>
      Browser automation is powerful, which means it deserves respect. Two things to internalize:
    </p>
  </div>

  <Callout variant="gold" title="1. Claude acts as you">
    <p>
      Same as connectors — it operates with your login and your permissions. If it submits a form,
      <em>you</em> submitted it. Claude will always ask before high-risk actions like making a purchase,
      publishing something, or sharing personal data — but you're accountable for the outcome.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <Callout variant="code" title="2. Watch for prompt injection">
    <p>
      This is the browser-specific risk. Malicious websites can hide instructions in a page trying to
      hijack what Claude does — the AI equivalent of a phishing email. Anthropic builds defenses against
      this and Claude blocks itself on genuinely risky sites, but the practical rule is simple:
      <strong>start with trusted sites, and review anything sensitive before you let Claude act.</strong>
      If Claude does something unexpected, pause and look.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <div class="prose-content">
    <p>
      You're not alone on this, either. Aptera IT is putting guardrails in place at the org level — the
      Chrome extension will be centrally managed, and certain high-risk systems (our financial, HR, and
      infrastructure tools) will be blocked from browser automation by policy. So the "be careful" advice
      has real backstops behind it, not just good intentions.
    </p>
  </div>

  <Quote attribution="Anthropic" source="Claude for Chrome safety guidance">
    "Only grant permissions to familiar websites while learning how Claude works. Always confirm before
    Claude handles financial, personal, or work-critical tasks."
  </Quote>

  <div class="prose-content">
    <p>
      It's a beta feature in the browser. Treat it like a sharp tool: incredibly useful, worth handling
      carefully. When in doubt, the
      <a class="text-gold underline underline-offset-2" href={href('/safety/')}>Safety &amp; Data</a> rules are
      your backstop.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 class="mb-3 mt-10 text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">Chrome + Office: one workflow, no handoff</h2>

  <div class="prose-content">
    <p>
      The real payoff is chaining Chrome together with the
      <a class="text-gold underline underline-offset-2" href={href('/m365/')}>M365 add-ins</a>. Chrome becomes
      the <em>research and gather</em> layer; the Office add-ins become the <em>produce the deliverable</em>
      layer — with no copy-paste seam in the middle:
    </p>
  </div>

  <Callout variant="gold" title="A real example, start to finish">
    <p><strong>1. In Chrome:</strong> "Pull the published pricing from these four competitor tabs into a table."</p>
    <p><strong>2. In Excel:</strong> "Drop that into a comparison model and calculate our position against each."</p>
    <p><strong>3. In PowerPoint:</strong> "Turn the Excel comparison into three slides for Thursday's review, using our template."</p>
    <p class="!mt-3">What used to be an afternoon of tab-juggling and reformatting becomes one connected flow.</p>
  </Callout>

  <KeyTakeaways>
    <ul>
      <li>Claude in Chrome works <strong>inside your browser</strong> — navigating, clicking, and filling forms on live pages, including tools that don't have a connector.</li>
      <li>It's different from pasting a link (it <em>operates</em> the page) and different from connectors (it's for sites <em>without</em> an API).</li>
      <li>The higher-leverage moves: multi-tab groups, recording a workflow once to replay it, and scheduled browser tasks.</li>
      <li><strong>Watch for prompt injection</strong> — start with trusted sites and review anything sensitive before letting Claude act.</li>
      <li>Claude acts as you and confirms before consequential actions; the <a class="text-gold underline underline-offset-2" href={href('/safety/')}>Safety &amp; Data</a> rules always apply.</li>
    </ul>
  </KeyTakeaways>

  <Callout variant="gold" title="📣 Getting set up">
    <p>
      Want the Chrome extension enabled, or hit something odd? Email
      <strong><a href="mailto:jake@aptera.us" class="text-gold underline underline-offset-2">jake@aptera.us</a></strong>.
    </p>
  </Callout>
</BaseLayout>
